1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,428
Good morning everyone.
Before we head out to

2
00:00:02,452 --> 00:00:04,952
the coast next week, I
want to run through the

3
00:00:05,139 --> 00:00:07,852
techniques you'll be
using on your first survey.

4
00:00:07,876 --> 00:00:10,772
We'll be working along a
200 metre section of shore

5
00:00:10,985 --> 00:00:13,755
just south of the
harbour wall, not the full

6
00:00:13,779 --> 00:00:16,752
kilometre we covered
last year. There are 24 of

7
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:19,976
you, so you'll be in
groups of four, each with its

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,772
own strip of shore. To
count species, we'll use

9
00:00:22,773 --> 00:00:25,908
a quadrat transect, a line
running down the shore

10
00:00:25,932 --> 00:00:29,092
with a square frame
placed at intervals along it.

11
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,583
The frames we use
are one metre square.

12
00:00:31,607 --> 00:00:34,672
The half metre ones are too
small for this kind of shore.

13
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:37,968
Before you place the
first frame, note the height

14
00:00:37,992 --> 00:00:40,832
of the tide, because what
you find depends on how

15
00:00:40,919 --> 00:00:43,898
far the water has gone
out. For water temperature,

16
00:00:43,922 --> 00:00:46,912
you'll use a simple
thermometer, and for salinity,

17
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:50,630
a refractometer. You used
one in the lab last term.

18
00:00:50,654 --> 00:00:53,920
Trainers are no good on
wet rock, so please wear

19
00:00:53,921 --> 00:00:57,473
waterproof boots. Pens
smudge when they get wet, so

20
00:00:57,497 --> 00:01:01,120
do all your recording in
pencil. Rock pools are best

21
00:01:01,121 --> 00:01:04,785
surveyed at low tide, when
most of the shore is exposed.

22
00:01:04,809 --> 00:01:08,092
If you can't identify
something, don't guess. Just

23
00:01:08,204 --> 00:01:11,441
photograph it, and we'll work
out what it is back in the lab.

24
00:01:11,465 --> 00:01:13,932
Some of the species
you'll find are protected,

25
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:17,079
and you must never
handle those. Each group will

26
00:01:17,103 --> 00:01:20,989
have a GPS unit to record the
exact location of each quadrat.

27
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:23,024
I'd also bring a spare notebook.

28
00:01:23,048 --> 00:01:26,880
Someone always drops one
in a rock pool. We'll still go if

29
00:01:26,881 --> 00:01:29,959
there's light rain, but if
storm warnings are issued,

30
00:01:29,983 --> 00:01:33,132
the fieldwork will be
cancelled. Each group also needs

31
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:37,625
a fully charged phone, for
photographs, and in case of emergency.

32
00:01:37,649 --> 00:01:40,252
Any algae samples
go in a sealed bag,

33
00:01:40,314 --> 00:01:43,200
so they don't dry out.
Your reports are due two

34
00:01:43,224 --> 00:01:47,326
weeks after the fieldwork, not
one, as it says in the old handbook.

35
00:01:47,327 --> 00:01:49,627
Put a ruler in every
photograph, so we

36
00:01:49,651 --> 00:01:53,047
can judge the size of what
you've found. And afterwards,

37
00:01:53,048 --> 00:01:56,480
I'll be in my office until 5pm
if you have any questions.

38
00:01:56,504 --> 00:02:00,000
Right, let's look at how to
fill in the the recording sheets.

